    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d265">

                <pb facs="0415" n="321" sameAs="frus:frus1924v02/pg321"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">815.113/50</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to President
                    Coolidge</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1924-03-22"><hi rendition="#italic">March 22,
                        1924</hi></date>.</dateline></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">My Dear Mr. President</hi>: The Department has just
                    received a telegram from Stauffer, Eshelman and Company, Limited, of New
                    Orleans, stating that a representative of the revolutionary forces in Honduras
                    has placed an order with them for twenty-six thousand dollars worth of rifles
                    and ammunition. The Company inquired whether it could make delivery of these
                    rifles and ammunition to the representative of the revolutionary forces at
                    shipside, New Orleans, or could make shipment direct to Honduras. It is
                    impossible to stop the shipment of such arms to Honduras unless a proclamation
                    prohibiting the exportation of arms and munitions of war is issued. A Joint
                    Resolution of Congress approved January 31, 1922, authorizes <pb facs="0416"
                        n="322" xml:id="pg322"/> the President to make such a proclamation and this
                    was done in the case of Mexico on January 7, of this year.<anchor n="24"
                        xml:id="d265-f1"/></p>
                <p>In view of the present chaotic conditions existing in Honduras I feel that such a
                    proclamation should immediately be issued and I am enclosing such a proclamation
                    herewith for your signature,<anchor n="25" xml:id="d265-f2"/> should it meet
                    with your approval.</p>
                <p>Faithfully yours,</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Charles E. Hughes</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
